{"traj_id": "numina-6929_3", "problem": "How many times should a die be thrown so that the probability of the inequality\n\n$$\n|m / n-1 / 6| \\leqslant 0.01\n$$\n\nis not less than the probability of the opposite inequality, where $m$ is the number of times one dot appears in $n$ throws of a die?", "gold": "632", "pred": "632", "is_correct": true, "tier": "hard", "n_tokens": 3151, "meta_leak": [], "paragraphs": ["Let me work through this problem carefully.\n\nWe're throwing a fair die $n$ times. Let $m$ be the number of times we get a \"one\" (one dot). We want to find $n$ such that:", "$$P\\left(\\left|\\frac{m}{n} - \\frac{1}{6}\\right| \\leqslant 0.01\\right) \\geqslant P\\left(\\left|\\frac{m}{n} - \\frac{1}{6}\\right| > 0.01\\right)$$", "The random variable $m \\sim \\text{Binomial}(n, p)$ where $p = 1/6$.", "We want:\n$$P\\left(\\left|\\frac{m}{n} - \\frac{1}{6}\\right| \\leqslant 0.01\\right) \\geqslant \\frac{1}{2}$$", "This is because the two events are complementary, and their probabilities sum to 1.", "So we need:\n$$P\\left(\\left|\\frac{m}{n} - \\frac{1}{6}\\right| \\leqslant 0.01\\right) \\geqslant 0.5$$", "The inequality $|m/n - 1/6| \\leqslant 0.01$ means:\n$$-0.01 \\leqslant \\frac{m}{n} - \\frac{1}{6} \\leqslant 0.01$$\n$$\\frac{1}{6} - 0.01 \\leqslant \\frac{m}{n} \\leqslant \\frac{1}{6} + 0.01$$\n$$0.1567 \\leqslant \\frac{m}{n} \\leqslant 0.1833$$", "So we need $P(0.1567n \\leqslant m \\leqslant 0.1833n) \\geqslant 0.5$.", "Since $m \\sim \\text{Binomial}(n, 1/6)$, we have:\n- $E[m] = n/6$\n- $Var(m) = n \\cdot (1/6) \\cdot (5/6) = 5n/36$\n- $\\sigma = \\sqrt{5n/36} = \\frac{\\sqrt{5n}}{6}$", "Using the normal approximation:\n$$\\frac{m - n/6}{\\sqrt{5n}/6} \\approx N(0,1)$$", "The inequality becomes:\n$$P\\left(\\frac{0.1567n - n/6}{\\sqrt{5n}/6} \\leqslant \\frac{m - n/6}{\\sqrt{5n}/6} \\leqslant \\frac{0.1833n - n/6}{\\sqrt{5n}/6}\\right) \\geqslant 0.5$$", "Let me compute the bounds:\n- Lower: $(0.1567 - 1/6) \\cdot n / (\\sqrt{5n}/6) = (1/6 - 0.01 - 1/6) \\cdot 6\\sqrt{n}/\\sqrt{5} = -0.01 \\cdot 6\\sqrt{n}/\\sqrt{5} = -0.06\\sqrt{n}/\\sqrt{5}$", "Wait, let me redo this more carefully.\n\n$\\frac{m}{n} - \\frac{1}{6} \\leqslant 0.01$ means $\\frac{m - n/6}{n} \\leqslant 0.01$", "So $\\frac{m - n/6}{\\sqrt{5n/36}} \\leqslant \\frac{0.01n}{\\sqrt{5n/36}} = \\frac{0.01n \\cdot 6}{\\sqrt{5n}} = \\frac{0.06n}{\\sqrt{5n}} = \\frac{0.06\\sqrt{n}}{\\sqrt{5}}$", "So the z-value is:\n$$z = \\frac{0.06\\sqrt{n}}{\\sqrt{5}} = \\frac{0.06\\sqrt{n}}{2.2361} = 0.02683\\sqrt{n}$$", "We need:\n$$P(-z \\leqslant Z \\leqslant z) \\geqslant 0.5$$", "where $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\n$P(-z \\leqslant Z \\leqslant z) = 2\\Phi(z) - 1 \\geqslant 0.5$", "$2\\Phi(z) \\geqslant 1.5$", "$\\Phi(z) \\geqslant 0.75$", "$z \\geqslant \\Phi^{-1}(0.75)$", "$\\Phi^{-1}(0.75) \\approx 0.6745$", "So:\n$$0.02683\\sqrt{n} \\geqslant 0.6745$$", "$$\\sqrt{n} \\geqslant \\frac{0.6745}{0.02683} = 25.14$$", "$$n \\geqslant 632$$\n\nLet me double-check: $0.6745 / 0.02683 = 25.14$... Let me verify: $0.02683 \\times 25 = 0.67075$, $0.02683 \\times 25.14 = 0.6745$. Yes.", "$n \\geqslant 25.14^2 = 632.0$", "So $n \\geqslant 632$.\n\nWait, let me be more precise. $\\Phi^{-1}(0.75)$.", "The standard normal CDF: $\\Phi(0.67) \\approx 0.7486$, $\\Phi(0.675) \\approx 0.7501$.", "Actually, $\\Phi^{-1}(0.75) \\approx 0.67449$.", "So $z \\geqslant 0.67449$.", "$0.02683\\sqrt{n} \\geqslant 0.67449$", "$\\sqrt{n} \\geqslant 25.139$", "$n \\geqslant 631.98$", "So $n \\geqslant 632$.\n\nHmm, let me recalculate more carefully.", "$z = \\frac{0.06\\sqrt{n}}{\\sqrt{5}}$", "$z \\geqslant 0.6745$", "$\\sqrt{n} \\geqslant \\frac{0.6745 \\sqrt{5}}{0.06} = \\frac{0.6745 \\times 2.23607}{0.06} = \\frac{1.50830}{0.06} = 25.138$", "$n \\geqslant 631.94$", "So $n \\geqslant 632$.\n\nThe answer is $n = 632$.", "Wait, but I should double-check whether the problem is asking for the probability to be \"not less than\" (i.e., $\\geqslant$) the probability of the opposite inequality. Yes, that's what I computed. The probability of the event should be $\\geqslant 0.5$.", "Actually, let me reconsider. The problem says \"the probability of the inequality $|m/n - 1/6| \\leqslant 0.01$ is not less than the probability of the opposite inequality.\" The opposite inequality is $|m/n - 1/6| > 0.01$. These two events partition the sample space (assuming continuity, which the normal approximation gives us). So:", "$P(|m/n - 1/6| \\leqslant 0.01) \\geqslant P(|m/n - 1/6| > 0.01)$", "$P(|m/n - 1/6| \\leqslant 0.01) \\geqslant 1 - P(|m/n - 1/6| \\leqslant 0.01)$", "$2P(|m/n - 1/6| \\leqslant 0.01) \\geqslant 1$", "$P(|m/n - 1/6| \\leqslant 0.01) \\geqslant 1/2$", "This is what I had. So the answer is $n = 632$.", "Let me verify: $n = 632$:\n$z = 0.06\\sqrt{632}/\\sqrt{5} = 0.06 \\times 25.1397/2.23607 = 0.06 \\times 11.2427 = 0.67456$", "$\\Phi(0.67456) \\approx 0.75$", "$P = 2(0.75) - 1 = 0.5$ ✓", "For $n = 631$:\n$z = 0.06\\sqrt{631}/\\sqrt{5} = 0.06 \\times 25.1197/2.23607 = 0.06 \\times 11.2339 = 0.67403$", "$\\Phi(0.67403) \\approx 0.7497$", "$P = 2(0.7497) - 1 = 0.4994 < 0.5$ ✗\n\nSo the minimum $n$ is 632."], "answer_text": "# Finding the Minimum Number of Die Throws\n\n## Setting Up the Problem\n\nLet $m$ be the number of times \"one\" appears in $n$ throws. Then $m \\sim \\text{Binomial}(n, p)$ with $p = 1/6$.\n\nThe condition is:\n$$P\\!\\left(\\left|\\frac{m}{n}-\\frac{1}{6}\\right|\\leqslant 0.01\\right) \\geqslant P\\!\\left(\\left|\\frac{m}{n}-\\frac{1}{6}\\right|> 0.01\\right)$$\n\nSince these two events are complementary, this simplifies to:\n$$P\\!\\left(\\left|\\frac{m}{n}-\\frac{1}{6}\\right|\\leqslant 0.01\\right) \\geqslant \\frac{1}{2}$$\n\n## Normal Approximation\n\nThe binomial variables are:\n$$E[m] = \\frac{n}{6}, \\qquad \\text{Var}(m) = n\\cdot\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot\\frac{5}{6} = \\frac{5n}{36}, \\qquad \\sigma_m = \\frac{\\sqrt{5n}}{6}$$\n\nThe inequality $|m/n - 1/6| \\leqslant 0.01$ becomes:\n$$\\left|\\frac{m - n/6}{\\sigma_m}\\right| \\leqslant \\frac{0.01\\,n}{\\sqrt{5n}/6} = \\frac{0.06\\,n}{\\sqrt{5n}} = \\frac{0.06\\sqrt{n}}{\\sqrt{5}}$$\n\nDefining the critical z-value:\n$$z = \\frac{0.06\\sqrt{n}}{\\sqrt{5}}$$\n\n## Solving for $n$\n\nWe need:\n$$P(-z \\leqslant Z \\leqslant z) \\geqslant 0.5 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad 2\\Phi(z)-1 \\geqslant 0.5 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\Phi(z)\\geqslant 0.75$$\n\nThis gives:\n$$z \\geqslant \\Phi^{-1}(0.75) \\approx 0.6745$$\n\nSolving:\n$$\\frac{0.06\\sqrt{n}}{\\sqrt{5}} \\geqslant 0.6745$$\n\n$$\\sqrt{n} \\geqslant \\frac{0.6745\\sqrt{5}}{0.06} = \\frac{0.6745 \\times 2.2361}{0.06} = \\frac{1.5083}{0.06} \\approx 25.14$$\n\n$$n \\geqslant 25.14^2 \\approx 632$$\n\n## Verification\n\n| $n$ | $z = \\frac{0.06\\sqrt{n}}{\\sqrt{5}}$ | $P = 2\\Phi(z)-1$ | Satisfies? |\n|-----|--------------------------------------|-------------------|------------|\n| 631 | 0.6740 | ≈ 0.4994 | ✗ |\n| **632** | **0.6746** | **≈ 0.5000** | **✓** |\n\nAt $n = 632$, the probability of the desired inequality equals (just reaches) $\\frac{1}{2}$, satisfying the \"not less than\" condition.\n\n$$\\boxed{632}$$", "annotations": {"27B·v4": {"nodes": [{"node_id": 0, "p_start": 0, "p_end": 1, "label": "SU", "deps": [], "branch": 0, "path": "M", "flags": ""}, {"node_id": 1, "p_start": 2, "p_end": 5, "label": "CP", "deps": [0], "branch": 0, "path": "M", "flags": ""}, {"node_id": 2, "p_start": 6, "p_end": 7, "label": "CP", "deps": [1], "branch": 0, "path": "M", "flags": ""}, {"node_id": 3, "p_start": 8, "p_end": 8, "label": "CP", "deps": [1], "branch": 0, "path": "M", "flags": ""}, {"node_id": 4, "p_start": 9, "p_end": 10, "label": "CP", "deps": [2, 3], "branch": 0, "path": "M", "flags": ""}, {"node_id": 5, "p_start": 11, "p_end": 11, "label": "CP", "deps": [4], "branch": 0, "path": "A", "flags": ""}, {"node_id": 6, "p_start": 12, "p_end": 12, "label": "MB", "deps": [5], "branch": 0, "path": "A", "flags": ""}, {"node_id": 7, "p_start": 13, "p_end": 14, "label": "CP", "deps": [4,